A||||1||StuGood||Coolant Lines to Throttle Body on V6||||5||
Z||000000||StuGood||09-13-2001||11:32 AM||||This thought occurs to me everytime I have to R&R the throttle body on a Fiero 2.8-liter V6.  The coolant lines are so stiff, it tends to be a pain to get them onto the TB.  And then the lines sometimes tend to fight you while you're trying to not crossthread the fittings.    <P>There's a flat welded steel "bridge" between the two lines, close to where they come out from under the plenum and head forward towards the TB.  The simplest mod I can think of, to give the lines more, well, "give," is to cut that "bridge," and replace it with a removeable clamp that would allow the lines to move independently (i.e. - slide through the clamp) until you retightened it.<P>Any comments?  Is there something I'm not thinking of?  Has someone already tried this?  <P>Looks like GM took some pains to restrain those lines, and I assume they had good reason to.  I've read other posts where people have done away with the coolant lines entirely.  That's fine, but I'm not interested in doing that, FWIW.<BR>        <P> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||09-13-2001||01:33 PM||||The strap is the result of vibration testing. The lines probably fractured during tests.<P>If you've ever enjoyed an iced throttle body, don't remove the lines. I had one ice up and stick. Just not any fun at all.<P>It you don't drive in or experience freezing temperatures there, you'd probably be ok.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||1
